 = sum(alloc.values())\\n    return {\\n        \\\"allocations\\\": alloc,\\n        \\\"name_mapping\\\": name_mapping,\\n        \\\"total\\\": total\\n    }\"\n\nNow, to make this a valid JSON string, we must put it inside double quotes and escape any double quotes that are part of the string. So we need to replace every \" with \\\". Let's do that. I'll go through the string and replace. Since the string contains double quotes in lines like raise ValueError(\"No segments\") -> after replacement: raise ValueError(\\\"No segments\\\"). Also the f-strings: raise ValueError(f\\\"Budget {budget} less than total minimum {total_min}\\\") becomes raise ValueError(f\\\"Budget {budget} less than total minimum {total_min}\\\") but note the backslashes before quotes. Also the return dict has double quotes: \"allocations\", \"name_mapping\", \"total\". So those become \\\"allocations\\\", \\\"name_mapping\\\", \\\"total\\\". Also we need to escape any backslashes that might appear? There are no backslashes originally. So the final string will have many backslash-quote sequences.\n\nI'll manually write the escaped version. To avoid mistakes, I'll copy the above and use search and replace in my mind. But since this is text, I'll write it carefully.\n\nThe final JSON object will be: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped code>\"}\n\nI'll now write the escaped code string. I'll use double quotes for the entire string, so inside I need to escape double quotes.\n\nLet me write it line by line with \\n and \\\" as needed.\n\nLine1: def solve(case):\\n\nLine2:     from fractions import Fraction\\n\nLine3:     segments = case['segments']\\n\nLine4:     budget = case['budget']\\n\nLine5:     # Validate\\n\nLine6:     if not segments:\\n\nLine7:         raise ValueError(\\\"No segments\\\")\\n -> becomes: raise ValueError(\\\"No segments\\\") but in JSON we need to escape the inner double quotes: raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"No segments\\\\\\\")? Actually double quotes inside JSON string need to